# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored.

# Matches docker-compose.yml. Change if you point at a different Postgres.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://ecommerce:ecommerce@localhost:5432/ecommerce"

# Better Auth. Generate a fresh secret per environment:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3300"

# --- Transactional email ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Two providers, and lib/email/index.ts prefers Gmail when it is configured.
#
# Gmail is the default because of the situation rather than any superiority:
# Resend only sends from a domain whose DNS you have verified, and a Render
# subdomain is not one you own. Gmail sends as the account that consented, so
# it needs no domain. Both are HTTP APIs — there is no SMTP adapter, because
# Render blocks outbound SMTP.
#
# Set EITHER the three GMAIL_* below, OR the Resend pair. EMAIL_FROM is needed
# by both. Without either, the console adapter prints mail to the terminal
# locally and REFUSES to send in production — a loud failure rather than order
# confirmations quietly going nowhere.
#
# Get the refresh token from a one-time consent:
#   GMAIL_CLIENT_ID="…" GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET="…" pnpm tsx scripts/gmail-authorise.mts
#
# PUBLISH the project first — Google Auth Platform → Audience → Publish app.
# While it is in "Testing" Google expires refresh tokens seven days from
# consent, and publishing afterwards does not rescue a token already minted
# under that rule. Publish, then mint.
GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=""
GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=""
GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=""
#
# Setting these has a second effect worth knowing about: `requireEmailVerification`
# in lib/auth/index.ts follows whether email is configured, so the moment these
# are filled in, verification becomes mandatory to sign in. Any existing account
# with email_verified = false is locked out until it verifies. Check the count
# before switching this on in an environment that already has users.
#
# Email verification is also what attaches a guest's past orders to their new
# account (lib/orders/claim.ts), so without a provider that path never fires.
# The better answer once a real domain exists: bounce handling, higher limits,
# and it does not tie the shop's mail to one person's Google account.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# The sender. On Gmail, the address that granted consent — Gmail rewrites From:
# to match the authorised account, so anything else here is ignored rather than
# honoured. On Resend, an address on a domain verified with them.
EMAIL_FROM=""
# Where a buyer's reply goes. Falls back to EMAIL_FROM. Point it at an inbox a
# person actually reads; orders@ usually is not one.
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=""

# --- Google media connections ----------------------------------------------
#
# Encrypts the OAuth client secrets and refresh tokens in store_google_*.
# Base64 of 32 random bytes — it must decode to exactly 32 or lib/media/crypto.ts
# throws:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Optional so the app boots without it; a store that has never connected Google
# never touches it. Rotating it strands every existing connection — the
# ciphertext becomes undecryptable and every seller has to reconnect.
GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# Bearer token for POST /api/media/sweep, which reconciles uploads with Google.
# Absent closes the endpoint rather than opening it. Minimum 16 characters.
#
# There is no cron in this project, so something external has to poke it — a
# scheduled GitHub Action is the cheapest thing that actually runs.
MEDIA_SWEEP_SECRET=""

# --- Observability ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Absent means disabled, which is right for a local checkout — nobody needs
# their test orders in a dashboard.
SENTRY_DSN=""
# Written into every structured log line, so environments are separable.
# One of: development | preview | production
APP_ENV=""

# --- Jomma: bKash / Nagad settlement verification -------------------------
#
# All three or none. The app boots without them and simply does not offer
# bKash or Nagad at checkout; set one or two and the client throws on first
# use rather than half-working. See docs/jomma.md.
#
# JOMMA_URL is not optional in practice. The SDK falls back to a default host
# when it is missing, and every call then fails looking like a network fault
# rather than a missing variable.
JOMMA_URL=""
# Server-side only. This authorises creating payment requests against a real
# receiving account — it must never reach a browser bundle.
JOMMA_API_KEY=""
# Signs inbound webhooks. A different value from JOMMA_API_KEY.
JOMMA_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# --- Cloudflare R2: product images ----------------------------------------
#
# All five or none. Without them the app falls back to writing images under
# public/uploads, which is fine locally and broken on any serverless host — the
# filesystem is per-instance and does not survive a redeploy.
#
# R2_PUBLIC_URL's hostname is read by next.config.ts and added to the <Image>
# allowlist automatically, so these two can never drift apart.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=""
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
R2_BUCKET=""
# The r2.dev domain from the bucket's public-access settings, or your own.
# No trailing slash.
R2_PUBLIC_URL=""
